<pdd-reason>The gate between an unscreened pitch and the public board. A pure verdict function, so the daemon that calls it stays free of judgement logic and an LLM can replace the stub without touching the queue loop.</pdd-reason>

<pdd-interface>
{"type":"module","module":{"functions":[
  {"name":"Verdict","signature":"@dataclass(frozen=True) class Verdict","returns":"feature_id: str; passed: bool; reason: RejectionReason | None; detail: str"},
  {"name":"screen_pitch","signature":"def screen_pitch(pitch: Mapping[str, Any]) -> Verdict","returns":"the decision for one pitch; never raises for content reasons"}
]}}
</pdd-interface>

<pdd-dependency>prompts/shared/constants_python.prompt</pdd-dependency>

Implement `orchestrator/screener.py`. Step 1 of US-02 is deterministic — no LLM, no network. The
LLM gatekeeper arrives in step 2 behind this exact signature.

<responsibility>
Decide whether one pitch may become a public feature request, and say why when it may not.
</responsibility>

<non_responsibilities>
This module DOES NOT read or write Redis — the caller owns the queue and every key.
This module DOES NOT touch Postgres, insert a row, or know that Postgres exists.
This module DOES NOT publish events, notify an author, or decide a rejected pitch's fate.
This module DOES NOT dequeue or retry; the daemon owns the loop.
This module DOES NOT call an LLM in step 1 — the seam exists so step 2 can, unchanged.
</non_responsibilities>

<vocabulary>
**Verdict** — the whole output. `passed=True` means the pitch may become a `VOTING` row; `passed=False` means it is dropped and never persisted anywhere.
**Pure function** — same input, same verdict, no I/O. That is what lets this be tested without a queue, a database, or a model, and what lets the daemon treat screening as one call.
</vocabulary>

<capabilities>
- MAY inspect the pitch's `title` and `description`.
- MAY return any `RejectionReason` member from `shared.constants`.
- MUST NOT perform I/O of any kind — no socket, file, database, or model call.
- MUST NOT mutate the pitch it is given.
</capabilities>

<contract_rules>
R1 (MUST): Return a `Verdict` for every input, including malformed ones. A pitch missing `title` or `description`, or carrying non-string values, is `passed=False` with reason `unclear` — never an exception, because the daemon's only alternatives are to crash the loop or invent a policy of its own.
R2 (MUST): Take the reason from the `RejectionReason` enum in `shared.constants` verbatim — `security`, `off_topic`, `unclear`. A bare string such as `"unsafe"` or `"incoherent"` MUST NOT be returned; those are not members and would break every consumer that compares against the enum.
R3 (MUST NOT): Return `already_shipped` or `merged`. Both are dedup outcomes owned by the PM agent (US-03), so emitting them here would misattribute a dedup result to the safety gate.
R4 (MUST): In step 1, pass every structurally valid pitch. This screener exists to prove the pipeline, so it rejects only what it can judge without a model: missing or non-string fields, and text outside the frozen `openapi.yaml` bounds — title 1–60, description 30–300.
R5 (MUST): Echo the input `feature_id` onto the verdict, so the daemon can correlate a decision with the queue item it came from without threading the pitch through separately.
R6 (MUST): Keep `detail` short, factual, and free of the pitch's own text. It is an operator log line, not author-facing copy, and unscreened content must not travel into logs (US-02 keeps it out of every durable store).
R7 (MUST NOT): Read configuration, the environment, or module-level mutable state. A verdict depends only on its argument, so two callers in one process cannot influence each other.
R8 (MUST): Keep `screen_pitch` synchronous. It performs no I/O, so `async def` would advertise a suspension point that does not exist and force every future caller to await it.
</contract_rules>

<pdd>
Step 2 replaces the body of `screen_pitch` with an LLM gatekeeper judging safety and topical
relevance against the target app, and R4 relaxes accordingly. The signature, the `Verdict` shape,
and R1–R3 plus R5–R8 are written to survive that change untouched, so neither the daemon nor its
tests move.

When the LLM lands it can fail — a refusal, a malformed reply, an exhausted retry. The rule then
is fail-closed: no verdict means no `passed=True`, because admitting unscreened content because a
model was unavailable is the single outcome US-02 exists to prevent. Step 1 has no such failure
mode, so that rule is deliberately not written yet.
</pdd>

